---
/**
 * Editorial policy.
 *
 * Owner: trust-compliance-officer (docs/agents/ROSTER.md §3). This page RENDERS editorial policy;
 * it does not author it. The workflow states are DIRECTIVE.md §18.6, the publication checks are
 * §19, and the rights-language discipline is AGENTS.md §1.3 and DIRECTIVE.md §3.5. Where
 * docs/EDITORIAL_POLICY.md (owner: content-editorial-lead) states something this page does not, the
 * page is wrong and gets changed — not the policy.
 *
 * Current-state claims verified against the repository at the reviewed date: the passenger-rights
 * collection in apps/web/src/content/ holds articles at status `source_review` and `publishable`
 * and none at `published`; the source registry at data/rights/sources/registry.json records its
 * publication gate as closed, with no source verified and every fetch attempt refused by the build
 * environment's network policy; the served sitemap lists only the routes that exist and no article.
 * The review intervals named below (30, 90, 180 days) are the intervals those files actually carry,
 * read from their reviewedAt and nextReviewDue fields, not a cadence invented here.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'

const title = 'Editorial policy — DelayPilot'
const description =
  'How DelayPilot writes, sources, reviews, and corrects guidance: seven workflow states, primary sources only, and why no article is published yet.'

/**
 * DIRECTIVE.md §18.6, in order. The gate text answers one question per state: what must be true
 * before an article may leave it.
 */
const workflow = [
  {
    state: 'Draft',
    gate: 'The article has a distinct question to answer, an answer-first opening that answers it in the first paragraph, and original explanation rather than a rewrite of a regulator page. Every factual claim is tagged with the source it will be checked against. A draft with a claim and no candidate source does not advance; the claim is cut.',
  },
  {
    state: 'Source review',
    gate: 'Every factual claim traces to a primary official source that has been opened and read, recorded in the source registry with the date it was verified. A rule value — a threshold, an amount, a deadline, an effective date — may only come from the rule set that cites those verified sources. A claim that cannot be sourced is removed, not softened into a vaguer version of itself.',
  },
  {
    state: 'Legal and factual review',
    gate: 'The language is checked against the rules that keep guidance honest: only the five permitted rights statuses, outcomes phrased as what may apply, no statement that a payment is due or that an airline is required to pay, no future rule applied before its verified effective date, enforcement guidance never presented as a repeal, and context — nearby weather, an airline-stated reason, an airspace event — never rendered as a proven legal cause. The required disclaimers are present next to the thing they qualify.',
  },
  {
    state: 'Publishable',
    gate: 'The article passes the publication checks: enough original content for its type, its sources cited and current, a unique title, description, and opening, a canonical URL, a reviewed date, no placeholder text, no private data, and no data shown under a licence that does not permit showing it. It is ready, and waiting for a human read.',
  },
  {
    state: 'Published',
    gate: 'It is visible and in the sitemap. This is the only state that enters the sitemap; an article in any other state is not offered to search engines, however finished it looks.',
  },
  {
    state: 'Review due',
    gate: 'Its next-review date has passed. The article stays visible — a correct article does not become wrong at midnight — but it is queued for re-verification against its sources, and re-verification means opening them again rather than trusting the last reading.',
  },
  {
    state: 'Stale',
    gate: 'Re-verification did not happen in time, or a source moved materially and the article has not caught up. A stale article leaves the sitemap and stops being indexable until it has been through source review again.',
  },
]
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>Editorial policy</h1>

    <p>
      <strong>
        Every DelayPilot article moves through seven states, and only one of them — published —
        reaches a search engine.
      </strong> The states exist to stop a plausible sentence from becoming a published fact. Right now
      no article has reached that last state, and the reason is on this page rather than hidden behind
      it.
    </p>

    <p class="meta">Reviewed <time datetime="2026-09-12">12 September 2026</time>.</p>

    <h2>The seven states</h2>

    {
      workflow.map((step) => (
        <>
          <h3>{step.state}</h3>
          <p>{step.gate}</p>
        </>
      ))
    }

    <h2>Primary sources, or nothing</h2>

    <p>
      A rule value is only ever taken from the body that made the rule: the regulator, the official
      journal, the legislative text, or the aviation authority's own published guidance. News
      coverage, law-firm commentary, claims-company marketing, forum posts, and aggregator summaries
      are never the authority for a threshold, an amount, a deadline, or an effective date. They are
      often how a change is noticed; they are never how it is confirmed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Each source is recorded with its canonical URL, the date it was last opened, and the date it
      is next due for review. An article cites the rule set, and the rule set cites the source, so a
      correction at the source propagates to every page that depends on it instead of being fixed on
      one page and missed on four others.
    </p>

    <h2>No invented statistics</h2>

    <p>
      No percentage, average, success rate, accuracy figure, user count, review score, or savings
      claim appears in DelayPilot's writing unless it comes from a named source or from a measured,
      published method. Where a number would be convincing but unverified, the article says what is
      not known instead. This applies to our own product claims as forcefully as to anyone else's:
      DelayPilot makes no superlative claim about itself — no ranking, no accuracy boast, no
      comparison it has not measured — and does not market intelligence it has not validated.
    </p>

    <h2>How rights are described</h2>

    <p>
      Passenger-rights writing reports which of five statuses a situation reaches under a dated rule
      set: a rule likely applies, may apply, is not indicated, cannot be determined from what is
      known, or is a future rule that is not yet in force. None of those means money is due. An
      article never states that a payment is due to a traveler, never states that an airline is
      required to pay, and never converts context into cause — nearby weather does not prove that a
      disruption was outside an airline's control, and an airline's stated reason is reported as the
      airline's statement, not as a finding.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two consequences of that discipline are worth naming, because they are where most published
      guidance goes wrong. A reform that has been adopted but has not taken effect is described as
      adopted and not yet in force, with its timeline, and is never applied to a flight that
      happened before its effective date. And a regulator's decision about where to direct its
      enforcement attention is described as enforcement guidance, never as a repeal of the
      underlying rule.
    </p>

    <h2>Review cadence</h2>

    <p>
      Every article and every source carries a reviewed date and a next-review date. The interval is
      30, 90, or 180 days, chosen by how fast the underlying material can change: 30 days where a
      rule is actively moving or an enforcement position is live, 90 days for a settled regulatory
      framework, 180 days for method and explanation that does not depend on a changing rule. When
      the date passes, the article becomes review due; if the re-check does not happen, it becomes
      stale and leaves the sitemap.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections and retractions</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A factual error is corrected as soon as it is verified</strong>, and the correction
        is stated on the page: what was wrong, what it now says, and the date it changed. A silent
        edit of a factual claim is not a correction.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A wrong rule value is fixed in the rule set first</strong>, not in the article. The
        article re-derives from the corrected rule set, so every other page carrying that value is
        fixed in the same change.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An article that cannot be corrected is retracted rather than deleted</strong>, with
        the reason stated in its place, so that anyone who acted on it can see what changed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong
          >A correction that could have changed someone's decision is treated as an incident</strong
        >, not as an edit: what shipped, who could have relied on it, and what prevents a repeat.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      DelayPilot publishes no contact address today, so there is currently no route for a reader to
      report an error. That is a gap, it is stated rather than papered over, and this page will name
      the address on the day one exists.
    </p>

    <h2>How this writing is produced</h2>

    <p>
      DelayPilot's articles are drafted with automated assistance and then checked against primary
      sources by a reviewer, whose name and review date are recorded with the article. No claim,
      number, date, or citation enters an article because a language model produced it: a model can
      draft a sentence, but only an opened primary source can make it publishable. DelayPilot does
      not sell automation as its value proposition, and does not market the model behind a sentence,
      because what matters to a traveler is whether the sentence is true.
    </p>

    <p>
      No article is commissioned, sponsored, reviewed, or influenced by an advertiser or a
      commercial partner. There are none today, and when there are, the separation is the point of
      the{' '}
      <a href="/advertising-policy/">advertising policy</a> and the{' '}
      <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the content stands today</h2>

    <p>
      The passenger-rights explainers and the launch guides are written, and every one of them is
      held at source review. They are held there for a specific and checkable reason: <strong
        >not one primary source has been opened</strong
      >. The source registry lists the regulator pages each rule depends on, records the date each
      fetch was attempted, and records that every attempt was refused by the network policy of the
      environment this site is built in. Its publication gate is closed, so no rule value has a
      verified citation behind it, and nothing that depends on one may be published.
    </p>

    <p>
      The one article at publishable makes no claim about any regulator's text — it explains how
      DelayPilot itself reaches a rights status — which is why it can clear source review with no
      external citations at all.
    </p>

    <p>
      The consequence is visible rather than hidden: no article has reached published, none is
      listed in the sitemap, and no rule set is in force. A page that has not been published is kept
      out of the sitemap and marked not to be indexed, whatever state it is otherwise in. When the
      sources can be opened and verified, the articles move through the remaining states in the
      order above, and this page records the date that happened.
    </p>

    <h2>Related policies</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/terms/">Terms of use</a> — why passenger-rights output is an estimate rather than a
        determination.
      </li>
      <li><a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a> — what reading these pages collects about you.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
